{
 "version": 1,
 "title": "Advanced 9 \u00b7 Drums from scratch",
 "text": "No samples: a kick and a snare built from an oscillator and noise. The kick is a sine an octave below the bass, whose pitch drops fast at the start of each hit: one envelope with a 45 ms DECAY into its FM (2.5 octaves) does the drop, another shapes its volume. That drop is what makes a sine sound like a kick: the 'click' and 'thud' are the same sweep. The snare is two layers: noise through a band-pass (the rattle of the snares) plus a low triangle (the drum's body), struck by a short envelope. The Clock plays the kick on every beat; a Sequencer stepping on eighths with steps 3 and 7 on plays the snare on 2 and 4.\n\nTry: the kick's pitch envelope DECAY (longer: a long, booming drop; shorter: a tight click), its FM (how far it drops), its TUNE; the snare's filter CUTOFF and the mixer's body level; switch other sequencer steps on.",
